Among the prior works, speed–accuracy trade-offs for GNNs have been attacked from three main angles: (1) mini-batch neighbour or sub-graph sampling (GraphSAINT, Bandit Samplers, Thanos, ANS-GT), (2) quantisation / sparsification of the model itself (Degree-Quant, A2Q, GDC), and (3) randomised approximation of sparse matrix multiplications in the backward pass of non-attentive GNNs (RSC).  None of these papers deals with the computational bottleneck that is unique to attention-based GNNs: the scatter-add of edge messages whose gradients must be multiplied by stored attention weights.  The proposed ARSC-GAT is the first method that
• constructs an unbiased, variance-controlled Monte-Carlo estimator specifically for the backward scatter-gather kernel of GAT-style layers;
• derives an importance score that can be computed for free from quantities already resident in GPU memory (‖∂L/∂h_i‖ and α_ij), avoiding an extra forward pass;
• re-adapts RSC’s layer-wise FLOP-budget allocation and epoch-wise caching to a different primitive (scatter instead of SpMM) and proves its compatibility with attention weights;
• keeps the forward path exact, maintaining identical predictions and avoiding the bias problems reported for biased samplers such as Thanos;
• plugs into existing PyG layers through a 200-line autograd wrapper, making it orthogonal to and combinable with neighbour-sampling or quantisation techniques discussed in related work.
Because none of the listed related works approximates the *backward* attention scatter operation, and because adapting RSC to this qualitatively different kernel is non-trivial (edge-wise rather than column-row sampling, per-target-node top-k, re-weighting for unbiasedness), the contribution is genuinely new.
